---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import ProviderCard from "../../components/ProviderCard.astro";
const popularityOrder = ["paddle", "lemon-squeezy", "gumroad", "polar", "fastspring", "stripe-managed-payments", "dodo-payments", "creem"];
const popularityRank = new Map(popularityOrder.map((slug, index) => [slug, index]));
const providers = (await getCollection("providers"))
  .map((entry) => entry.data)
  .filter((provider) => provider.model === "merchant-of-record")
  .sort((a, b) => (popularityRank.get(a.slug) ?? Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER) - (popularityRank.get(b.slug) ?? Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER) || a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const marketScenarios = [
  { key: "us", label: "US market", setup: "USD sale → USD bank" },
  { key: "eu", label: "EU market", setup: "EUR sale → EUR bank" },
  { key: "other", label: "Outside US/EU", setup: "CHF sale → CHF bank" }
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout title="Merchant of Record providers — Choose a Payment Processor" description={`Compare ${providers.length} Merchant of Record providers for software, SaaS, and digital products.`}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Path 01 · Provider is seller" title="Merchant of Record providers" description="The provider becomes the documented seller for approved transactions. Compare the exact responsibility boundary, not only the headline fee." meta={`${providers.length} provider profiles · independently reviewed`} />
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell provider-grid">
      {providers.map((provider) => <ProviderCard provider={provider} />)}
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="currency-comparison" class="section section-tint currency-comparison-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading-inline currency-comparison-heading">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Multi-currency reality</span><h2>“Local currency” can mean three different things.</h2></div>
        <p>A localized number is not necessarily the currency charged to the buyer, held in your balance, or paid to your bank. Follow the full money path and check who pays each conversion.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="currency-reading-key" aria-label="How to read a money path">
        <span><b>1 · Checkout</b>Currency the buyer actually pays</span>
        <span><b>2 · Balance</b>Currency the provider records for you</span>
        <span><b>3 · Payout</b>Currency that reaches your bank</span>
        <p><strong>Count each arrow where the currency changes.</strong> Two changes mean two conversions, although the provider may not publish a separate fee for both.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="regional-example-heading">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Three market examples</span><h3>US, EU, and everywhere else.</h3></div>
        <p>Currency—not geography alone—determines the route. These representative USD, EUR, and CHF examples assume the buyer and seller bank use the same currency, isolating provider FX from buyer-card FX.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap regional-example-wrap">
        <table class="data-table regional-example-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Provider</th>{marketScenarios.map((market) => <th><span>{market.label}</span><small>{market.setup}</small></th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {providers.map((provider) => provider.currencySupport && <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={`/providers/${provider.slug}/`}>{provider.name}</a><small>{provider.currencySupport.moneyPath}</small></th>
              {marketScenarios.map((market) => {
                const example = provider.currencySupport!.marketExamples.find((item) => item.market === market.key)!;
                const countLabel = example.conversions === "not-disclosed" ? "FX count not disclosed" : `${example.conversions} currency changes`;
                return <td data-market={`${market.label} · ${market.setup}`}><code>{example.path}</code><b class:list={{ "conversion-count": true, "is-zero": example.conversions === "0", "is-unknown": example.conversions === "not-disclosed" }}>{countLabel}</b><small>{example.cost}</small></td>;
              })}
            </tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="currency-example-disclaimer">These are routing examples, not net-payout quotes. They exclude the headline transaction fee, payout service fees, bank charges, and exchange-rate movement.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data-table currency-comparison-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Buyer experience</th><th>Seller balance and payout</th><th>FX cost</th><th>Money path</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {providers.map((provider) => provider.currencySupport && (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><a href={`/providers/${provider.slug}/`}>{provider.name}</a></th>
                <td><strong>{provider.currencySupport.presentment}</strong><span>{provider.currencySupport.customerCharge}</span></td>
                <td>{provider.currencySupport.settlement}</td>
                <td><span><b>Buyer:</b> {provider.currencySupport.buyerFx}</span><span><b>Seller:</b> {provider.currencySupport.merchantFx}</span></td>
                <td><code>{provider.currencySupport.moneyPath}</code></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="currency-warning">
        <strong>Watch the two-conversion routes.</strong>
        <div>
          <p>FastSpring explicitly documents that sales may be converted into USD for reporting and then converted again for a non-USD payout. Lemon Squeezy likewise processes transactions and payouts in USD before a selected non-USD bank payout is converted. In both cases, “shown in local currency” does not mean the full route stays in that currency.</p>
          <p><b>Polar has a related—but not identical—path:</b> the buyer is genuinely charged in an enabled local currency, then Polar converts the sale to its USD balance and Stripe may convert it again for a non-USD payout. Polar publishes the second conversion at 0.25% in the EU or up to 1% elsewhere, but does not separately state a fee for the first conversion.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-blue">
    <div class="shell flow-section">
      <div><span class="eyebrow eyebrow-inverse">Responsibility boundary</span><h2>What usually moves—and what does not.</h2></div>
      <div class="boundary-list">
        <div><span>Provider</span><strong>Legal sale, covered indirect-tax calculation and remittance, payment processing, transaction fraud and disputes</strong></div>
        <div><span>Shared</span><strong>Billing questions, refunds, invoices, customer communication, evidence, privacy, and incident response</strong></div>
        <div><span>Your business</span><strong>Product, delivery, product support, entitlements, bookkeeping, income tax, vendor oversight, and excluded transactions</strong></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell service-models">
      <span class="eyebrow">Tradeoffs to model</span><h2>The fee buys an operating role—not magic.</h2>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div><strong>Eligibility</strong><p>MoRs approve sellers and products. Services, marketplaces, physical goods, regulated categories, and some billing flows may be excluded.</p></div>
        <div><strong>Buyer identity</strong><p>The MoR entity can appear on receipts, invoices, card statements, terms, and support communications.</p></div>
        <div><strong>Cash timing</strong><p>You receive net payouts under the provider schedule rather than settling each payment directly to your merchant balance.</p></div>
        <div><strong>Dependence</strong><p>Checkout, subscriptions, tax evidence, customer records, and payment credentials can become expensive to move later.</p></div>
      </div>
      <a class="card-link" href="/learn/">Explore the full payments library →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="shell note-grid">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">Important</span><h2>MoR does not mean “nothing left to do.”</h2></div>
      <div class="prose"><p>You still own the product, product support, entitlements, bookkeeping, income tax, vendor oversight, and every responsibility outside the contract. Coverage can also change by seller country, buyer location, and product type.</p><a class="card-link" href="/guides/merchant-of-record-vs-payment-processor/">Understand the boundary →</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
